---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";
import Sidebar from "../components/sidebar.astro";
---

<Layout title="About | Glen Meredith">
    <div class="flex h-screen">
        <Sidebar currentPage="about" />

        <div class="flex-1 bg-white p-8 overflow-auto">
            <div id="content">
                <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto py-16 px-6">
                    <!-- Main Title -->
                    <h1
                        class="text-6xl md:text-7xl font-black mb-16 leading-tight"
                    >
                        About
                    </h1>

                    <!-- The Honest Version -->
                    <div class="mb-16">
                        <h2 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-8">
                            The Honest Version
                        </h2>

                        <div
                            class="space-y-6 text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"
                        >
                            <p>
                                Here's the thing: UX is the only subject that's
                                ever been able to keep my attention. And
                                specifically, it's the research-driven side of
                                UX that hooked me.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                I don't want to be someone who designs websites
                                and apps purely off aesthetics, vibes, and
                                "industry best practices." Don't get me
                                wrong—best practices matter—but I've always been
                                frustrated by designs that are just "what the
                                designer thought was best." I believe design
                                should be backed by research. Not "I'm the
                                designer, I know best," but "here's what the
                                data shows users actually need."
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                That's why the Family Promise project is one of
                                my favorites. I got to use card sorting to
                                understand users' mental models, then design a
                                sitemap based on actual patterns in the data.
                                That process—research informing design—is where
                                I thrive.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Honestly? Aesthetics and picking the perfect
                                colors, fonts, and shapes is probably my weak
                                point. I've always struggled with making things
                                look effortlessly beautiful. But what I'm good
                                at is designing based on what works best in each
                                situation—for the user, for the content, and for
                                the medium.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Currently -->
                    <div
                        class="mb-16 bg-green-50 rounded-xl p-8 border-l-4 border-green-600"
